# Tinda Repo — Round 2 Fix Instructions

Good progress on the round-1 fixes — those are confirmed working (UserType enum,
Firestore getters returning `id`, `listJobs` filtering, middleware file/export
naming, AuthContext consolidation, login page, dependency cleanup all check out).

This round found new bugs — some are follow-through gaps from the round-1
fixes, others were introduced by the large feature expansion (resumes, mock
interviews, offers, referrals, notifications, company reviews, candidate
notes, etc.). Fix in this order.

---

## 1. CRITICAL: `auth()` still called with no arguments in 7 places

`src/lib/auth.ts`'s `auth()` function requires a `Request` (or cookies object)
argument to extract the session token — it returns `null` immediately if
nothing is passed. But these call sites were never updated to pass `request`:

- `src/app/api/jobs/route.ts` — line 36 (`POST`)
- `src/app/api/jobs/[id]/route.ts` — line 37 (`PUT`) and line 88 (`DELETE`)
- `src/app/api/profiles/employer/route.ts` — line 22 (`GET`) and line 31 (`PUT`)
- `src/app/api/profiles/candidate/route.ts` — line 27 (`GET`) and line 36 (`PUT`)

**Fix:** Change every `await auth()` in these files to `await auth(request)`,
matching the pattern already used correctly in `src/app/api/swipes/route.ts`
and `src/app/api/matching/route.ts`. Note: the `GET` handlers in the profile
routes currently don't even accept a `request` parameter — add it to the
function signature (`export async function GET(request: Request)`).

After fixing, manually verify: log in as an employer, create a job, edit it,
delete it, and load/save your employer + candidate profile pages. All of
these are currently broken (silently return 401).

---

## 2. CRITICAL: `firestore.rules` has broken brace nesting — will fail to deploy

Run `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` (or open the file in the Firebase
console rules editor) and you'll see it rejected. The structural problem:
after the `jobAlerts` match block, the `/databases/{database}/documents` block
gets closed one brace too early. Then `resumes` is declared directly under
`service cloud.firestore` (invalid — all `match` blocks must live inside the
`documents` block). Then `service cloud.firestore` itself gets closed early
too, and `mockInterviews`, `companyReviews`, `offers`, `candidateNotes`, and
`referrals` are declared completely outside any `service` block.

**Fix:** Rewrite `firestore.rules` so there is exactly one
`service cloud.firestore { match /databases/{database}/documents { ... } }`
wrapper, with every collection's `match` block — `users`, `candidateProfiles`,
`employerProfiles`, `jobs`, `swipes`, `matches`, `conversations`, `messages`,
`notifications`, `badges`, `swipeAnalytics`, `interviews`, `assessments`,
`assessmentResults`, `savedJobs`, `jobAlerts`, `resumes`, `mockInterviews`,
`companyReviews`, `offers`, `candidateNotes`, `referrals` — nested inside it.
Validate brace balance before committing (or just run it through the Firebase
emulator/console, which will reject malformed rules immediately).

---

## 3. CRITICAL: Employer profile ID vs. employer user ID confusion

This is a data-modeling bug spanning multiple files. `employerProfiles` now
has an auto-generated Firestore doc ID (separate from the employer's actual
user/auth ID), looked up via a `userId` field. `jobs.employerId` is set to
this profile doc ID (`employerProfile.id`) — that part is internally
consistent for job ownership. The bug is everywhere else this ID gets
confused with the employer's real user ID:

**3a. `getEmployerProfile(job.employerId)` always returns `null`**
`getEmployerProfile()` queries `where('userId', '==', ...)`, but
`job.employerId` holds the *profile doc ID*, not a `userId`. Since profile
doc IDs are auto-generated and never equal any `userId` value, this query
never matches. Two call sites are broken because of this:
- `src/app/api/swipes/route.ts` line ~150 (inside the POST handler, the
  "candidate swipes a job → check if employer already liked them → create
  match" flow) — this flow can currently never fire.
- `src/app/api/cover-letter/route.ts` line ~28

**Fix:** Both call sites should use `getEmployerProfileById(job.employerId)`
instead — this function already exists in `src/lib/firebase.ts` but is
currently unused anywhere in the codebase. Import and use it.

**3b. Matches/conversations/notifications are filed under the wrong ID for employers**
In `src/app/api/swipes/route.ts`, when a match is created, these all use
`employerProfile.id` (the profile doc ID) where they should use the
employer's actual user ID (the same `user.id` used for the candidate side):
- `createMatch({ ..., employerId: employerProfile.id })`
- `createConversation({ ..., participant2Id: employerProfile.id })`
- `createNotification({ userId: employerProfile.id, ... })` (both
  occurrences, in the "candidate likes job" and "employer likes candidate"
  branches)

But `listNotifications(session.user.id)` (in `src/app/api/notifications/route.ts`)
and the Firestore rules for `matches`/`conversations` all check against the
real authenticated user's UID. Since nothing is ever created under that UID
for the employer side, **an employer will never see their own match
notifications or conversations** — they're silently filed under an ID nobody
queries by.

**Fix:** Decide on one consistent convention. Recommended: `matches`,
`conversations`, and `notifications` should always store the real user
ID (Firebase Auth UID) for both parties, never the `employerProfile` doc ID.
Only `jobs.employerId` needs to reference the profile doc ID (since that's
what job-ownership checks compare against). Audit every `createMatch`,
`createConversation`, and `createNotification` call in
`src/app/api/swipes/route.ts` and update them to pass `user.id` (for the
current employer) rather than `employerProfile.id`. Also double check
`src/app/api/chat/[conversationId]/route.ts` and any other file that reads
`participant1Id`/`participant2Id`/`employerId` on these collections for the
same mix-up.

---

## 4. Operator-precedence bug breaks the match counter

`src/app/api/swipes/route.ts`, inside the POST handler:
```ts
await updateUserFields(user.id, { totalMatches: (await getUserById(user.id))?.totalMatches || 0 + 1 });
```
`+` binds tighter than `||` in JS, so this evaluates as
`totalMatches || (0 + 1)`, i.e. `totalMatches || 1`. If the current value is
falsy (`0` or `undefined`), it always becomes exactly `1`. If it's already a
truthy number, it never increments at all.

**Fix:**
```ts
const currentUser = await getUserById(user.id);
await updateUserFields(user.id, { totalMatches: (currentUser?.totalMatches || 0) + 1 });
```

---

## 5. Missing Firestore composite indexes for new collections

`firestore.indexes.json` only defines indexes for `jobs`, `swipes`, `matches`,
and `users`. But `src/lib/firebase.ts` now runs compound queries (multiple
`.where()` calls and/or `.where().orderBy()` combos) against several
collections with no matching index defined:
- `notifications` — `.where('userId','==',...).orderBy('createdAt','desc')`
- `offers` — `.where(field,'==',userId).orderBy('createdAt','desc')`
- `referrals` — `.where('referrerId','==',...).orderBy('createdAt','desc')`
- `candidateNotes` — `.where('employerId','==',...).where('candidateId','==',...).orderBy('createdAt','desc')`
- `companyReviews` — `.where('employerId','==',...).orderBy('createdAt','desc')`
- `mockInterviews` — `.where('candidateId','==',...).orderBy('createdAt','desc')`
- `messages` (in `markMessagesRead`) — `.where('conversationId','==',...).where('senderId','!=',...).where('read','==',false)` — note this combines a `!=` filter with equality filters, which has extra Firestore index requirements

Each of these will throw a `FAILED_PRECONDITION: The query requires an index`
runtime error the first time it executes in production (Firestore normally
gives you a console link to auto-create the missing index when this happens
in development — but that's not something you want surfacing to real users).

**Fix:** Add composite index entries to `firestore.indexes.json` for each
collection/query pattern above. Test each new feature (notifications page,
offers, referrals, candidate notes, company reviews, mock interviews, marking
a chat as read) against the Firestore emulator or a dev project to catch any
that still error, then run `firebase deploy --only firestore:indexes`.

---

## 6. `firebase-admin` in Edge-runtime middleware

`src/middleware.ts` imports `auth` from `@/lib/auth`, which imports from
`./firebase/admin`, which initializes `firebase-admin`. Next.js middleware
runs on the **Edge runtime** by default, and `firebase-admin` depends on
Node.js-only APIs — this combination is a known source of build/runtime
failures ("A Node.js API is used which is not supported in the Edge Runtime").

**Fix:** Either:
- (a) Opt the middleware into the Node.js runtime, if your Next.js version
  supports it (check `export const config = { runtime: 'nodejs' }` support
  for middleware in Next 16 — this was stabilized in recent versions), or
- (b) Do lightweight cookie-presence checking only in middleware (no token
  verification) and rely on the API routes' own `auth(request)` calls (which
  run in the Node.js runtime already) to do full token verification.
  Middleware would just check "does a `__session` cookie exist" for the
  redirect logic, not fully verify it.

Verify by actually running `npm run build` and `npm run dev`, then hitting a
protected route, to confirm middleware doesn't throw at runtime.

---

## 7. Minor: possible login redirect race condition

In `src/app/(auth)/login/page.tsx`, after a successful
`signInWithEmailAndPassword`, `router.push("/dashboard")` fires immediately.
But the `__session` cookie is set asynchronously inside
`AuthContext`'s `onAuthStateChanged` listener (it awaits `getIdToken()`
first). If middleware evaluates the `/dashboard` request before that cookie
is written, the user gets bounced back to `/login` right after a successful
login.

**Fix:** Either await the cookie being set before navigating (e.g., expose a
promise/flag from `AuthContext` that resolves once the cookie sync completes
after sign-in), or have the login page get the ID token and set the cookie
itself immediately after `signInWithEmailAndPassword` succeeds, before
calling `router.push`.

---

## Verification checklist

1. Log in as an employer: create, edit, and delete a job. Confirm none of
   these return 401.
2. Load and save both the candidate and employer profile edit pages.
3. `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules --dry-run` (or paste into the
   Firebase console rules editor) — confirm it's accepted.
4. As a candidate, swipe "like" on a job whose employer has already liked
   you back — confirm a match + conversation + notification is created and
   that **both** parties (not just the candidate) can see it.
5. Swipe several matches and confirm `totalMatches` on the user doc actually
   increments each time, not just jumping to 1.
6. Exercise every new feature at least once against a real (or emulated)
   Firestore project — notifications, offers, referrals, candidate notes,
   company reviews, mock interviews, marking a chat read — and confirm none
   throw a "query requires an index" error.
7. Run `npm run build` and `npm run dev`, then hit any protected route, to
   confirm middleware doesn't crash due to `firebase-admin` on the Edge
   runtime.
8. Log in with email/password and confirm you land on `/dashboard` reliably,
   without being bounced back to `/login`.
